  "prompt": "You are an associate analyst on the Westlake Capital Markets industrials research team. Managing Director Lin Wei-chen has asked you to build a QoQ margin variance analysis for Deere & Company (DE) following the latest 10-Q filing on May 11, 2026. This will support upcoming client conversations and needs to be client-presentation quality.\n\nPull the current-quarter figures from EDGAR for the quarter ending 2026-02-01. Revenues were $9,611M and net income was $656M (diluted EPS of $2.42). Note that some of the XBRL tags in the filing include historical comparatives — cross-check the gross profit and operating income figures against the actual 10-Q tables rather than relying on what might be prior-period data embedded in the extract. The attached DE_historical_financials.xlsx has five-year summary data to support prior-quarter pulls.\n\nBuild the analysis in Excel with the following structure:\n\n**Summary sheet** — Executive overview with QoQ gross margin, operating margin, net margin, and EBITDA margin. Call out the single biggest margin variance driver at the top. All dollar figures in millions with one decimal; present both percentage-point and basis-point changes.\n\n**Actual vs Budget sheet** — Waterfall-style variance bridge from prior-quarter margins to current-quarter margins. Quantify dollar and bps drivers across each P&L line item (revenue, COGS, gross profit, SG&A at $972M, R&D at $554M, operating income, net income). Reference Deere's three reportable segments (Production & Precision Agriculture, Small Agriculture & Turf, Construction & Forestry) where segment data is available in the filing.\n\n- Flag and isolate any one-time or non-recurring items distorting the comparison\n- Include EBITDA margin as a supplemental metric given industrials coverage conventions\n- Reconcile all figures back to EDGAR XBRL tags with source attribution\n- Build input cells so the model refreshes cleanly when the next quarterly filing drops\n- Document assumptions and data sources within the workbook\n\nFormat consistent with Westlake house conventions for research support materials.",
